data.content:
Here are the three breaking changes in EF Core 10 and their fixes:

---

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — now accepts a regular lambda (not an expression tree)

Your manual expression-tree building **won't compile** because `ExecuteUpdateAsync` now takes `Func<…>` instead of `Expression<Func<…>>`.

**Fix** — replace with the much simpler imperative style:

```csharp
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

---

## 2. Complex type column names are now uniquified / nested paths changed

Two related changes:
- **Same-level duplicates** get a numeric suffix (`Street` → `Street1`) to prevent silent column sharing.
- **Nested complex types** now use the full path: `Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City` maps to column `Payment_BillingAddress_City` (was `BillingAddress_City`).

**Fix** — pin existing column names explicitly:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
});

// For nested complex types, pin the old shorter name:
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>()
    .ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment)
    .ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress)
    .Property(a => a.City)
    .HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City");
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

All methods (`CommandReaderExecuting`, `CommandReaderExecuted`, `CommandScalarExecuting`, etc.) gained a `string logCommandText` parameter (contains redacted SQL for logging).

**Fix** — add the parameter:

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText)   // ← new parameter
    {
        // Use logCommandText for logging (sensitive data redacted)
        // Use command.CommandText for actual execution SQL
    }
}
```

---

**TL;DR**: Drop the expression-tree plumbing (it's now just imperative code), add `HasColumnName` calls to preserve existing column names, and add `string logCommandText` to your diagnostics logger methods.
